[{"page":1,"text":"Page 1 of 2; October 13, 2026, City Council Agenda\n\nNOTICE OF A REGULAR\n\nCITY COUNCIL MEETING\n\nOctober 13, 2026, at 6:00 PM\nPUBLIC NOTICE is hereby given that the Vineyard City Council will hold a regularly\nscheduled City Council meeting on Tuesday, October 13, 2026, at 6:00 PM, in the City Council\nChambers at City Hall, 125 South Main Street, Vineyard, UT. This meeting can also be viewed\non our live stream page.\n\nAGENDA\n\n1. CALL TO ORDER\n\n2. INVOCATION AND PLEDGE OF ALLEGIANCE (BY INVITATION)\n\n3. PUBLIC COMMENTS (3 MINUTES PER PERSON)\n“Public Comments” is defined as time set aside for citizens to express their views. Public\ncomments can be submitted ahead of time to robinr@vineyardutah.gov .\n\n4. WORK SESSION\n\n4.1 Discuss Short-Term Rentals (Ed Kennedy – 10 Minutes)\n\n5. CONSENT ITEMS\n\n5.1. Approval of the September 15, 2026, City Council Meeting Minutes\n\n6. APPOINTMENTS AND REMOVALS\n\n6.1. Appoint ARCH Commission Member (Brian Vawdreoy - 5 Minutes)\n\n7. BUSINESS ITEMS\n\n7.1. Approve RES 2026-40 - Awarding the Parking Enforcement Bid to EZ Park (5\n\nMinutes)\n\n7.2. Approve RES 2026-41 Establishing the Vineyard Economic Development\n\nPartnership (VEDP) (5 Minutes)\n\n7.3. Approve ORD 2026-12 Renaming the Active Transportation Commission (ATC)\nas the Pedestrian Safety Commission (PSC) (5 Minutes)\n\n7.4. Membership in the North Utah County Aquifer Council (NUCAC) (5 Minutes)\nConsideration and approval of a resolution authorizing Vineyard City's membership in\nthe North Utah County Aquifer Council (NUCAC), at an initial cost of $12,000 and\napproximately $6,000 annually thereafter. Page 1 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":2,"text":"Page 2 of 2; October 13, 2026, City Council Agenda\n\n7.5. Accept Resolution 2026-36: Opposing The Geneva Retention Pond Exchange And\n\nRequesting A Public Management Plan (5 Minutes)\n\n8. ADJOURNMENT\n\nThe next regularly scheduled meeting is on October 27, 2026.\nThis meeting may be held in a way that will allow a councilmember to participate electronically.\nThe public is invited to participate in all City Council meetings. In compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act, individuals\nneeding special accommodations during this meeting should notify the City Recorder at least 24 hours prior to the meeting by calling\n(385) 338-5183.\nI, the duly appointed City Recorder for Vineyard, Utah, hereby certify that the foregoing notice and agenda was posted at Vineyard City\nHall, on the Vineyard City and Utah Public Notice websites, and delivered electronically to staff and to each member of the Governing\n\nBody.\n\nAGENDA NOTICING COMPLETED ON:\n\nCERTIFIED (NOTICED) BY:\n\nOctober 9, 2026\nROBIN BOND, CITY RECORDER Page 2 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":3,"text":"Page 1 of 5; September 15, 2026, City Council Agenda\n\n1\n\nNOTICE OF A SPECIAL 2\n\nCITY COUNCIL MEETING 3\n\nSeptember 15, 2026, at 6:00 PM 4\n\n5\n\nPresent: 6\n\nMayor Zack Stratton 7\n\nCouncil Member Jacob Holdaway 8\n\nCouncil Member David Lauret 9\n\nCouncil Member Parker McCumber 10\n\nCouncil Member Ezra Nair 11\n\nCouncil Member Jacob Wood 12\nStaff Present: Chief Deputy Holden Rockwell with the Utah County Sherrif’s Office, City 13\nAttorney Jesse Riddle City Manager Brian Voeks, City Recorder Robin Bond, Community 14\nEnrichment Director Brian Vawdrey, Deputy City Recorder Tony Lara, Deputy Mayor David 15\nHerring, Finance Director Evan Smith, Media Specialist Brailee Tyler, and Public Works 16\n\nDirector/City Engineer Naseem Ghandour. 17\nAlso Present: Chief Marc Sanderson Orem Fire Department, Daria Evans Villas, John Pettingill 18\nHoldaway Fields, Darlene Price Waters Edge, Jesse Sandstrom Orchards, Abigail Sandstrom 19\nOrchards, Karen Cornelius Villas, Kim Cornelius Villas, Chip Price Providence, Barbara Porter 20\nHampton, Claudia Lauret Holdaway Road, David Pearce resident, and others who did not sign in 21\nor whose handwriting was illegible. 22\n\n23\n\nAGENDA 24\n\n25\n\n1. CALL TO ORDER 26\nMayor Stratton called the meeting to order at 6:02 pm. He roll-called the City Council and all 27\nmembers were present. 28\n\n29\n\n2. INVOCATION AND PLEDGE OF ALLEGIANCE (BY INVITATION) 30\n\nResident Barbara Porter gave the prayer and led the Pledge of Allegiance. 31\n\n32\n\n3. PUBLIC COMMENTS (3 MINUTES) 33\n“Public Comments” is defined as time set aside for citizens to express their views. Public 34\ncomments can be submitted ahead of time to robinr@vineyardutah.gov. 35\n\n36\nDaria Evans, Villas’ resident, expressed appreciation for Officer Kennedy and thanked City staff 37\nand the City Council for programs benefiting residents, including community events and suicide 38\nprevention efforts. 39\n\n40\nJohn Pettingill, Holdaway Fields resident, inquired about proposed amendments to the Holdaway 41\nFields development agreement. He noted residents received notice regarding proposed changes 42 Page 3 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":4,"text":"Page 2 of 5; September 15, 2026, City Council Agenda\nin February but had not received subsequent notifications despite the item being discussed over 43\nseveral months. He requested clarification regarding the public notification process. 44\n\nPublic comment was closed. 45\nAt the request of Council Member Nair, Fire Chief Marc Sanderson provided an update 46\nregarding emergency vehicle siren usage and concerns received from residents living near the 47\nnew fire station. 48\nChief Sanderson explained that Utah law currently requires emergency vehicles to utilize audible 49\nsirens and emergency lights when responding under emergency driving privileges. The 50\nrequirement is connected to governmental immunity protections and was recently highlighted 51\nthrough a Utah Court of Appeals decision involving emergency vehicle operations. 52\nChief Sanderson indicated local officials and municipalities may seek legislative changes during 53\nthe upcoming legislative session to address what appears to be a statutory drafting issue. He 54\nfurther explained the Fire Department intends to work with City communications staff to educate 55\nresidents regarding the legal requirements. 56\nCouncil members expressed appreciation for the clarification and encouraged public outreach on 57\nthe issue. 58\n\n4. WORK SESSION 59\n\n4.1 Interview Candidates for City Council Vacancy 60\nMayor Stratton reviewed the process for filling the vacancy created by the resignation of 61\nCouncilmember Ezra Nair. Each candidate was invited to provide remarks and answer questions 62\nfrom the Council. 63\n\nDavid Pierce highlighted: 64\n His service on the Planning Commission and Vineyard Cares Coalition. 65\n Military and federal government leadership experience. 66\n His commitment to communication, transparency, fiscal responsibility, planning, and 67\nservice delivery. 68\nCouncil questions and discussion focused on working relationships with fellow council 69\nmembers, resident engagement and communication, revenue generation and economic 70\ndevelopment, and problem-solving and constituent concerns. 71\n\nChip Price highlighted: 72\n Professional experience in information technology. 73\n His belief in representative government, truth-seeking, fiscal conservatism, and 74\ncommunity service. 75\n His desire to improve economic development opportunities within Vineyard. 76 Page 4 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":5,"text":"Page 3 of 5; September 15, 2026, City Council Agenda\nCouncil questions and discussion focused on budgeting and taxation, economic development and 77\nbusiness attraction, relocation of the railroad spur, and collaboration and decision-making. 78\n\nJesse Sandstrom highlighted: 79\n His extensive experience in public policy, civic engagement, and political technology. 80\n Family history of public service. 81\n Business leadership experience. 82\n His commitment to listening, collaboration, and long-term fiscal sustainability. 83\nCouncil discussion focused on economic development opportunities, fiscal sustainability, 84\nbuilding a stronger tax base, and objective decision-making and representative government. 85\nCouncilmembers expressed appreciation to all applicants for their willingness to serve. 86\n\nCandidate Laureana Lazarte was not present. 87\n\n88\nCity Recorder, Robin Bond, explained the selection process for the new council member. 89\n\n90\nCouncil Member Nair asked the other Council Members to share any insights they had about the 91\ncandidates. Council Member McCumber shared his insights on Mr. Pearce, Mr. Price, and Mr. 92\n\nSandstrom. 93\n\n94\n\n5. PROCLAMATIONS 95\n\n5.1. Charlie Kirk Day of Remembrance September 10, 2026 96\nMayor Stratton informed the Council that the Charlie Kirk Day of Remembrance Proclamation 97\nwas issued on September 10, 2026. The Mayor emphasized the importance of free speech, 98\npeaceful civic engagement, respect for differing viewpoints, and rejecting political violence. The 99\nproclamation was entered into the record. 100\n\n6. CONSENT ITEMS 101\n\n6.1. Approval of the September 8, 2026, City Council Meeting Minutes 102\n\n103\nMotion: Council Member McCumber motioned to approve the minutes as presented. Council 104\nMember Lauret seconded the motion . Yes: Council Members Holdaway, Lauret, McCumber, 105\n\nNair, and Wood. No : None. Motion Passed 5-0. 106\n\n107\n\n7. BUSINESS ITEMS 108\n\n7.1. Approve the First Amendment to the Holdaway Fields Development Agreement 109\nwith Cadence Homes and Goodboro Homes (Developers) 110\nCouncilmember Wood reviewed the proposed amendment and explained that it would provide 111\napproximately $1.3 million in additional impact fee revenue to the City while requiring the 112\ndeveloper to fund the demolition, grading, and cleanup of the East Park. He also noted that the 113\nagreement preserves several historic agricultural structures for future incorporation into a 114 Page 5 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":6,"text":"Page 4 of 5; September 15, 2026, City Council Agenda\nheritage park, provides for the completion of roadway, trail, park, and infrastructure 115\nimprovements, and allows the project to continue as a single-family residential development. 116\nStaff then presented additional details regarding the proposed amendment, including updated 117\nimpact fee provisions, modifications to the 400 South roadway design, adjustments to residential 118\nunit counts and development phasing, roadway connection requirements, and park improvements 119\nand land dedications associated with the project. 120\nCouncil discussed the negotiations, developer cooperation, and the benefits to both the City and 121\ntaxpayers. 122\nA minor amendment was also presented clarifying that certain housing allocations would be \"up 123\nto\" 22 units rather than a mandatory 22 units. 124\nMotion: Council Member Wood motioned to approve the First Amendment to the Holdaway 125\nagreement with the discussed changes. Council Member Lauret seconded the motion . Yes: 126\nCouncil Members Lauret, McCumber, Nair, and Wood. No : None. Abstained : Council Member 127\n\nHoldaway. Motion Passed 4-0. 128\n\n129\n\n7.2. Accept Quit Claim Deed for Holdaway Properties 130\nStaff presented quit claim deeds associated with Holdaway property transfers. Council discussed 131\nfuture sidewalk and bicycle boulevard improvements along Holdaway Road. 132\nMotion: Council Member McCumber motioned to direct the City to accept the quit claim deeds 133\nfor the Holdaway properties. Council Member Wood seconded the motion . Yes: Council 134\nMembers Lauret, McCumber, Nair, and Wood. No : None. Abstained: Council Member 135\n\nHoldaway. Motion Passed 4-0. 136\n\n137\n\n7.3. Appoint A City Treasurer 138\nFinance Director, Evan Smith discussed the statutory requirement for appointing a City 139\nTreasurer separate from the Finance Director position and outlined the Treasurer's 140\nresponsibilities, including oversight and custody of City financial assets and investment 141\naccounts. 142\nStaff and Mayor Stratton recommended appointing Dave Sanderson as City Treasurer. 143\nMotion: Council Member McCumber motioned to approve the Mayor’s appointment of Dave 144\nSanderson as the City Treasurer. Council Member Holdaway seconded the motion . Yes: 145\nCouncil Members Holdaway, Lauret, McCumber, Nair, and Wood. No : None. Motion Passed 5- 146\n\n0. 147\n\n148\n\n7.4. Select Candidate for City Council Vacancy 149\nThe City Recorder distributed paper ballots to Councilmembers. Following ballot tabulation 150\nconducted by City staff, a clear majority of three (3) votes was achieved. Mayor Statton 151 Page 6 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":7,"text":"Page 5 of 5; September 15, 2026, City Council Agenda\nannounced that Christopher \"Chip\" Price was selected to fill the vacant City Council seat for the 152\nremainder of the unexpired term. The Council congratulated Mr. Price. The oath of office was 153\nscheduled to occur following statutory notice requirements. 154\n\n7.5. City Council Annual Meeting Schedule Amendment #3 (Resolution 2026-35) 155\nCouncil reviewed Resolution 2026-35, which returns City Council meetings to a bi-weekly 156\nschedule beginning at 6:00 p.m. Council would meet the second and fourth Tuesdays of the 157\nmonth. 158\nMotion: Council Member Lauret motioned to accept the amended annual meeting schedule. 159\nCouncil Member Wood seconded the motion . Yes: Council Members Holdaway, Lauret, 160\n\nMcCumber, Nair, and Wood. No : None. Motion Passed 5-0. 161\nCouncil Member Nair delivered farewell remarks following his resignation from the City 162\nCouncil. He thanked residents for their civic engagement. He expressed appreciation for fellow 163\ncouncil members and staff and recognized the dedication and long hours contributed by City 164\nemployees. He shared that he would continue serving in public service through his position with 165\nProvo City. He reaffirmed his love and support for Vineyard City and its future. 166\nThe Council and audience expressed appreciation for his service. The City Recorder noted a 167\ncommemorative service gift would be presented once it arrived. 168\n\n8. ADJOURNMENT 169\n\nMayor Stratton adjourned the meeting at 7:51 pm. 170\n\n171\n\n172\n\n173\n\n174\n\nMINUTES PASSED ON :\n\nCERTIFIED (NOTICED) BY:\n\nROBIN BOND, CITY RECORDER\nSEAL Page 7 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":8,"text":"Name:\n\nVine\nyard\n\nResi\ndent\n\nFor how\nlong?\n\nWhy are you interested in serving on ARCH\n\nCommission?\n\nBryce Gandy Yes 9 years\n\nA couple of years ago I was asked to apply for the ARCH\n\nCommission and did, but was not selected at that time. I\nenjoy being involved in the community and love living\nhere in Vineyard. I want to see our community continue to\nbe a great place to be, with more opportunities for\neveryone to enjoy the arts, recreation, culture and\nheritage.\n\nAlexander Teemsma Yes 5 years\n\nI am a stay-at-home father with two children who\nregularly use the public parks in and around Vineyard. My\nchildren also participate in sports leagues organized by\n\nVineyard Parks and Recreation; I have coached and\nvolunteered as an assistant coach for many of my\nsonâ€™s teams. I am compelled to help make the\nprograms offered by Vineyard City more efficient and\nmore robust for both children and adults.\n\nWhen Vineyard City is ready to offer more programming\nand pursue new endeavors exemplifying the mission of\nthe ARCH Commission, I want to influence those efforts\nfor good. Until there are sufficient resources, I am happy\nto help with events, review ARCH grant applications, and\nproactively seek opportunities to grow the arts, bolster\nrecreation, highlight culture, and celebrate the heritage of\n\nVineyard City in a way that is fiscally responsible.\n\nAs an aside, I also support the City Council appointing a\nmember of the City Council to sit on the ARCH\nCommission. Page 8 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":9,"text":"Please describe any experience, background, or community\ninvolvement you have related to Arts, Recreation and Parks,\n\nCulture, and Heritage.\n\nARCH\n\nCommi\nssion\nmeetin\n\nDo\nyou\ncurre\nntly\n\nI've helped coach my children's soccer teams on occasion, regularly\nattend community events, and submit feedback when community\nresponses are asked for by the city. My professional background is in\nthe arts in filmmaking and advertising.\n\nYes No\n\nMy interests are not limited to sports. I also have a passion for the arts,\nculture, and the heritage of Vineyard City and the state of Utah. I take\nmy children to the BYU Museum of Art on a monthly basis for their Van\n\nGogh to Playdough clinics. My daughter is enrolled at classes at the\n\nOrem Recreation Center. We patronize libraries, museums, and parks\nall across the county on a regular basis.\n\nI have experience organizing with the Downtown Provo Art Stroll and\n\nMural Program, representing businesses and non-profits.\n\nMy wife, Jenny, is a Vice President at Miller Sports + Entertainment,\nwhich owns Real Salt Lake, Utah Royals FC, the Salt Lake Bees, and\n\nMegaplex. She does not have the time to serve on the commission but\nher network could be valuable to the ARCH Commission, depending\non the context.\nYes No Page 9 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":10,"text":"CITY OF VINEYARD, UTAH\n\nRESOLUTION NO. 2026-40\n\nA RESOLUTION AWARDING PARKING ENFORCEMENT SERVICES FOR THE\n\nPROVIDENCE AND SPRINGS NEIGHBORHOODS TO EZ PARK AND AUTHORIZING\n\nFINAL CONTRACT NEGOTIATIONS AND EXECUTION\nWHEREAS, Vineyard City provides for parking regulation and enforcement within certain residential areas of the City;\nWHEREAS, the City desires to continue parking enforcement services within the Providence Neighborhood and\nthe Springs Neighborhood;\nWHEREAS, the City conducted a competitive procurement and proposal evaluation process for parking enforcement\nservices;\nWHEREAS, EZ Park received the highest overall score through the City's proposal evaluation process, and the\nselection of EZ Park as the preferred provider was unanimous;\nWHEREAS, the Vineyard City Council finds that awarding the parking enforcement services bid to EZ Park is in the\nbest interests of the City and the residents of the affected neighborhoods; and\nWHEREAS, the City Council desires to authorize designated Council representatives, together with the City\nAttorney and appropriate City staff, to complete negotiations and finalize an agreement for such services.\n\nNOW, THEREFORE, BE IT RESOLVED BY THE VINEYARD CITY COUNCIL AS\n\nFOLLOWS:\n\nSECTION 1. AWARD OF BID.\nThe Vineyard City Council hereby awards the parking enforcement services bid for the Providence Neighborhood\nand the Springs\nNeighborhood to EZ Park, which received the highest overall proposal evaluation score and was unanimously selected\nas the preferred provider, subject to the negotiation and execution of a final written agreement.\n\nSECTION 2. AUTHORIZATION TO FINALIZE NEGOTIATIONS.\nCouncilmembers Wood and Price are hereby authorized to continue and finalize negotiations with EZ Park on\nbehalf of the City Council, in coordination with the Vineyard City Attorney and appropriate City staff.\nThe City Attorney and appropriate City staff are authorized to assist in the preparation, review, and finalization of the\nagreement and any related documents necessary to implement the parking enforcement services.\n\nSECTION 3. EXECUTION OF AGREEMENT.\nUpon completion of negotiations and approval of the final agreement as to legal form by the City Attorney, the Mayor\nis authorized to execute the agreement on behalf of Vineyard City without further action of the City Council.\n\nSECTION 4. ADMINISTRATIVE AUTHORITY.\nCity staff are authorized to take such administrative actions as may be reasonably necessary to implement the\nagreement and transition parking enforcement services to EZ Park.\n\nSECTION 5. EFFECTIVE DATE.\nThis Resolution shall become effective immediately upon passage and adoption. Page 10 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":11,"text":"PASSED AND ADOPTED by the Vineyard City Council this day of , 2026.\n________________________________________________________________________________________________________________\n\nAYE NAY ABSENT ABSTAIN\n\nJacob Holdaway\n\nDavid Lauret\n\nParker McCumber\n\nChip Price\n\nJacob Wood\n____________________________________________________________________________________________________________\n\nPresiding Officer\n\nMayor, Vineyard\n\nAttest\nCity Recorder, Vineyard Page 11 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":12,"text":"EZ PARK LLC | Proposal: City of Vineyard Residential Parking Program Confidential\n\nPage 1 of 11\n\nEZ PARK LLC\n\nDigital Parking Management & Enforcement\n\nPROPOSAL FOR\n\nResidential Parking Permit Management\nand Enforcement Services\n\nCity of Vineyard, Utah\n\nDate Submitted: July 1, 2026 | In Response to RFP Issued: June 29, 2026\n\nSUBMITTED BY\n\nCam Terry, CEO | EZ Park LLC\n(435) 231-3794 | Ezonboarding@ezparkllc.com | www.ezparkllc.com Page 12 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":13,"text":"EZ PARK LLC | Proposal: City of Vineyard Residential Parking Program Confidential\n\nPage 2 of 11\n\nEXECUTIVE SUMMARY\nEZ Park LLC is pleased to submit this proposal in response to the City of Vineyard's Request for Proposals\nfor Residential Parking Permit Management and Enforcement Services. We are a technology-driven\nparking management company operating across Utah, Oregon, Washington, California, and Texas, and\nwe currently serve residential communities within the City of Vineyard. That gives us direct, established\nknowledge of the community, its residents, and its parking dynamics.\nEZ Park's service model was built specifically around the values the City has articulated in this RFP:\nresident-first enforcement, transparency, progressive consequences, and the elimination of predatory\ntowing. Our license plate recognition (LPR) technology, fully digital permit platform, and proven\nprogressive enforcement protocol align precisely with the City's objectives.\nWe are operationally ready to implement within 30 days of contract award. Our existing Vineyard presence\nmeans there is no learning curve on local roads, neighborhoods, or resident expectations. We look forward\nto the opportunity to serve the City of Vineyard formally. Page 13 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":14,"text":"EZ PARK LLC | Proposal: City of Vineyard Residential Parking Program Confidential\n\nPage 3 of 11\n\n1. COMPANY INFORMATION\n\nCompany Overview\nEZ Park LLC is a digital parking management company founded to modernize residential and commercial\nparking through technology, fair enforcement, and a single point of accountability. We provide fully\nintegrated parking solutions, from digital permit issuance to LPR-powered nightly patrol enforcement,\nunder one vendor relationship. This eliminates the fragmented experience that comes with using separate\npermit software vendors and enforcement contractors.\n\nCompany Name EZ Park LLC\n\nHeadquarters Utah (serving UT, OR, WA, CA, TX)\n\nPrimary Contact Cam Terry, CEO\n\nPhone (435) 231-3794\n\nEmail Ezonboarding@ezparkllc.com\n\nWebsite www.ezparkllc.com\n\nCore Services Digital permit management, LPR patrol enforcement, booting, towing\ncoordination, paid visitor parking\n\nOrganizational Structure and Key Personnel\nEZ Park operates a lean, accountable structure with direct lines of responsibility from field operations to\nexecutive leadership. The City of Vineyard will have direct access to senior decision-makers rather than\nbeing routed through call centers or account managers removed from authority.\n• Cam Terry, CEO: Oversees all client relationships and contract performance. Primary executive\ncontact for the City.\n• Dave Terry, President: Dave brings 25 years of direct experience in the parking and recovery\nindustry to EZ Park. He founded and operated Statewide Recovery for 20 years, then owned and\noperated Zips Towing for an additional 5 years before selling both operations to focus full time on\nbuilding EZ Park LLC. That depth of hands-on operational experience is the foundation for\neverything EZ Park does, from how we train our drivers to how we designed our progressive\nenforcement model. When EZ Park says it understands enforcement, it is because our\nleadership has lived it.\n• Jaxton Reber, Chief Sales Officer: Jaxton brings extensive experience in sales and problem-\nsolving developed across a career as both a sales representative and a business owner. He\ncurrently owns and operates a resort in St. George, Utah, where he manages complex day-to-\nday operations and is responsible for ensuring every aspect of the business runs smoothly. That\ncombination of sales acuity and operational ownership gives Jaxton a practical understanding of\nwhat clients need and how to deliver it, skills he applies directly to building and maintaining EZ\n\nPark's client relationships.\n• Keaton Littlefield, Onboarding Coordinator: Keaton has founded and operated multiple\nbusinesses and specializes in ensuring smooth transitions and making sure clients are fully taken\ncare of at every stage. He is responsible for guiding new clients from contract signing through go-\nlive, coordinating system setup, resident communications, and City staff training so that nothing\nfalls through the cracks during implementation. Page 14 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":15,"text":"EZ PARK LLC | Proposal: City of Vineyard Residential Parking Program Confidential\n\nPage 4 of 11\n• Field Operations Team: Trained enforcement drivers operating LPR patrol vehicles on scheduled\nnightly routes. All personnel are thoroughly vetted and trained on EZ Park's progressive\nenforcement protocols.\n\nInsurance and Licensing\nEZ Park maintains the following insurance coverage and will provide current certificates of insurance to\nthe City upon request:\n• Commercial General Liability insurance at limits consistent with municipal contract requirements\n• Commercial Auto Liability insurance covering all patrol vehicles used in enforcement operations\n• Workers' Compensation insurance covering all EZ Park employees performing services under\nthis contract\nEZ Park operates in compliance with all applicable Utah licensing requirements for parking enforcement\nand vehicle immobilization services. Our towing partner operates under a valid Utah towing operator\nlicense. Documentation of licensing and insurance will be provided prior to contract execution.\n\n2. RELEVANT EXPERIENCE AND REFERENCES\n\nMunicipal and Residential Parking Experience\nEZ Park has extensive experience managing parking in residential communities across the Intermountain\nWest and Pacific Northwest, including apartment communities, townhome developments, and HOA-\ngoverned neighborhoods. Those are the same use cases the City of Vineyard is seeking to serve.\nNotably, EZ Park currently operates within the City of Vineyard, Utah, providing enforcement services to\nresidential communities in the immediate area. Our team already understands the road network, resident\nculture, and operational environment unique to Vineyard.\n\nActive Client References\nThe following clients have firsthand experience with EZ Park's digital permit platform and enforcement\nservices. We encourage the City to contact them directly.\n\nProperty Location Services Provided Contact\n\nRed Rock at Sienna\n\nHills\n\nWashington, UT Digital permits, LPR\nenforcement, booting\n\nEsmi Garcia\n\nLa Vida at Sienna Hills Washington, UT Digital permits, LPR\nenforcement, booting\n\nHarry Hanke\n\nDolan Plaza Portland, OR Digital permits, LPR\nenforcement, visitor\nparking\n\nJean Dolan\nFull contact information for each reference is available upon request. EZ Park welcomes direct\nconversation between City staff and our current clients.\n\nProperties Currently in Onboarding\nIn addition to our active client base, EZ Park has the following properties and municipal engagements\ncurrently in the onboarding or early implementation phase. This growth reflects both our operational\ncapacity and the confidence clients are placing in our model. Page 15 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":16,"text":"EZ PARK LLC | Proposal: City of Vineyard Residential Parking Program Confidential\n\nPage 5 of 11\n\nProperty / Entity Location Scope\n\nRexburg Municipality Rexburg, ID Municipal residential parking permit management\nand enforcement program\nLakefront at Vineyard Vineyard, UT Digital permit platform, LPR enforcement, booting\n\nParkway Lofts Utah Digital permits, LPR patrol enforcement\nEZ Park acknowledges that our completed reference portfolio is primarily composed of private residential\nand commercial communities rather than municipal jurisdictions. Our commercial portfolio includes retail\ncenters, business parks, and mixed-use properties where enforcement complexity is comparable to or\ngreater than residential programs, with higher vehicle turnover, more stakeholders, and less tolerance for\nerror. We want to be transparent about our municipal experience rather than obscure it. What we bring in\nplace of a long list of municipal contracts is something we believe is more relevant to this specific program:\nwe are already on the ground in Vineyard, we understand the community, and we are actively building a\nmunicipal track record with the Rexburg engagement that is structured identically to what the City of\nVineyard is seeking. We are not learning how to serve a municipality. We are doing it now.\nThe Rexburg engagement is directly comparable to the City of Vineyard's program in scope, structure,\nand stakeholder expectations. EZ Park is available to connect City staff with Rexburg personnel to discuss\nthe implementation experience and our performance to date.\n\n3. TECHNICAL SOLUTION\n\nA. Digital Permit Management Platform\nEZ Park provides a fully digital, cloud-hosted parking permit management platform purpose-built for\nresidential communities. The system requires no physical permits, hang tags, or stickers. Permits are tied\nentirely to license plate numbers and verified electronically during every patrol pass.\n\nPlatform Features\n• Online resident portal, accessible from any device and fully mobile-optimized\n• License plate-based permits with no physical pass required\n• Real-time permit database, always current and accessible to enforcement personnel in the field\n• Administrative dashboard: City personnel receive dedicated login access with full visibility into\npermit activity and enforcement records\n• Secure cloud-based architecture with data encrypted in transit and at rest\n• Complete audit trail covering every permit transaction, update, and enforcement action, all\ntimestamped and logged\n\nResident Self-Service Capabilities\n• Register and update vehicle information\n• Manage up to three (3) permits per household, or as modified by City policy\n• Request and issue guest and visitor permits\n• Request temporary permits for service vehicles, moving trucks, or special circumstances\n• Review full permit history and activity log\n• Receive automated notifications regarding permit status, renewals, and enforcement activity Page 16 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":17,"text":"EZ PARK LLC | Proposal: City of Vineyard Residential Parking Program Confidential\n\nPage 6 of 11\n\nB. Permit Structure\nAll permit parameters are controlled by City administrators through the dashboard. No system\nredevelopment is required to adjust permit policies.\n• Up to three (3) household permits per address, adjustable by City\n• Guest, short-term visitor, and temporary permits, all time-limited and issued by resident or City\nstaff\n• Special-event parking permits, configurable for City-designated events\n• Permit limits, durations, and categories all adjustable without vendor involvement\n\nC. Reporting and Analytics\nAll reports are exportable in CSV, PDF, and Excel formats and available on demand or on a scheduled\nbasis. The system covers every reporting requirement in the RFP:\n• Active permits and permit utilization by zone or neighborhood\n• Guest permit usage volume and frequency\n• Citations, booting activity, and towing activity, all with full supporting documentation\n• Enforcement trends, patrol response times, and coverage data\n• Resident complaints received and resolved, along with appeal submissions and outcomes\n\nMonthly summary reports are delivered automatically to City-designated contacts.\n\nD. Paid Visitor Parking\nIn addition to permit management and enforcement, EZ Park offers a fully integrated paid visitor parking\nsolution that can be deployed in designated neighborhood areas at the City's discretion. Rather than\ntreating visitor parking as a compliance problem, this system turns it into a revenue stream for the City.\nThe paid parking system works through QR code signs posted at visitor parking areas. Visitors scan the\ncode, enter their license plate, and pay for a time-limited parking session directly through the platform. No\nmeters, no payment kiosks, and no additional hardware are required. EZ Park's LPR patrol vehicles verify\npaid sessions automatically on every pass, the same way they verify resident permits.\nRevenue generated from paid visitor parking is remitted back to the City on a monthly basis. Based on EZ\nPark's experience across comparable residential programs, paid visitor parking typically generates\n$10,000 or more per year at active properties, which offsets a significant portion of or the entire monthly\nservice fee. In that scenario, the parking program effectively pays for itself.\nThe City has full control over which zones are designated for paid parking, pricing per session, and time\nlimits. EZ Park handles all payment processing, reporting, and enforcement. Paid parking can be enabled,\nadjusted, or disabled at any time through the administrative dashboard without system changes.\n\n4. ENFORCEMENT PLAN\n\nEnforcement Philosophy\nEZ Park's enforcement model is built on a simple belief: the goal of parking enforcement is compliance,\nnot revenue. Every element of our progressive protocol gives residents the opportunity to come into\ncompliance before escalating consequences are applied.\nA resident-first approach that prioritizes education, communication, and proportionate response reduces\ncomplaints, improves community relationships, and produces better long-term compliance than Page 17 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":18,"text":"EZ PARK LLC | Proposal: City of Vineyard Residential Parking Program Confidential\n\nPage 7 of 11\naggressive tow-first models. Property managers who have used tow-first vendors before switching to EZ\nPark routinely tell us they forgot parking was even an issue within 90 days of making the change.\nTo offer a concrete example of how this works in practice: across our active Utah residential portfolio, the\nlarge majority of vehicles flagged during patrol resolve their violation at the citation stage. Residents\nregister their vehicle, obtain a permit, or correct the issue before a second patrol runs. Boots are applied\nonly when a prior citation is documented in the system, and tows account for a small fraction of total\nenforcement actions, typically limited to vehicles with a pattern of documented non-compliance. Our\nexpectation on any new program is that towing volume approaches near-zero within the first 60 to 90 days\nas residents become familiar with the system. We view a high tow count as a sign that communication has\nfailed, not that enforcement is succeeding.\n\nProgressive Enforcement Protocol\n\nStep 1: Citation (First Violation)\nEvery first-time violating vehicle receives a citation. No boot, no tow. Citations are vendor-issued and\nclearly marked as such. They are not represented as criminal notices, civil penalties, or municipal\nviolations of any kind.\nEach citation includes the specific parking violation, applicable restrictions for the area, instructions for\nregistering a vehicle or obtaining a permit, EZ Park contact information, and the notice date, time, vehicle\ndescription, and license plate.\nCitations are printed on paper and placed under the windshield wiper or tucked into the driver's door jamb.\nNo adhesive material of any kind is applied to the vehicle. Notices can be removed by hand with no tools,\nsolvents, or scraping required.\n\nStep 2: Booting (Second and Subsequent Violations)\nFor vehicles with a documented prior warning on record, EZ Park may apply a wheel immobilization\ndevice. Booting is always prioritized over towing when both options are available.\n• Boot fee: $75, paid directly to release the vehicle\n• Response time: vehicle released within one (1) hour of payment confirmation\n• All booting incidents are photographed, timestamped, and documented in the enforcement\nsystem\n• City staff are notified of all booting activity in real time through the administrative dashboard\n\nStep 3: Towing (Chronic Non-Compliance or Safety Circumstances)\nTowing is reserved for cases of chronic non-compliance, specifically vehicles with repeated documented\nviolations and a prior booting history, or for the immediate safety circumstances outlined in the RFP,\nincluding fire lane violations, blocked emergency access, ADA violations, and obstructed roadways.\nPermitted vehicles will not be towed under any circumstances without explicit authorization from City Code\nEnforcement or law enforcement. All towing is coordinated through a licensed Utah tow company in full\ncompliance with applicable Utah statutes.\n\nAnti-Predatory Towing Commitments\n• No towing without a prior documented citation on record, except in immediate safety\ncircumstances\n• No booting or towing of permitted vehicles without City authorization\n• No financial incentive structures that reward towing volume\n• No EZ Park surcharges above standard tow company rates\n• Full photographic documentation of every enforcement action available to City staff within 24\nhours\n• Immediate suspension or reversal of any enforcement action upon City directive Page 18 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":19,"text":"EZ PARK LLC | Proposal: City of Vineyard Residential Parking Program Confidential\n\nPage 8 of 11\n\nCoordination with City Personnel\n• Dedicated point of contact for City personnel available by phone and email during all business\nhours\n• Emergency contact line staffed during all active patrol hours\n• Monthly enforcement summary reports delivered to designated City contacts\n• Prompt notification within two hours of any significant enforcement incident\n• Real-time dashboard access for City staff to monitor enforcement activity at any time\n\nAppeals and City Oversight\nAppeals are not controlled by EZ Park. The City retains final authority over all enforcement decisions. EZ\nPark's system provides the following documentation for every enforcement action to support City-\nadministered review:\n• Time-stamped photographs of the vehicle and violation\n• Enforcement officer notes and incident description\n• Full vehicle and license plate information\n• Complete permit status history for the vehicle\n• All prior warning and enforcement records\nUpon receiving a City directive to reverse or suspend an enforcement action, EZ Park will act immediately\nby removing boots, suspending towing coordination, or facilitating vehicle release. No enforcement action\nwill be maintained over City objection.\n\n5. DATA SECURITY AND PRIVACY\n\nData Use Restrictions\n• Data used solely for performance of the parking management and enforcement contract\n• Resident data will never be sold to third parties\n• Resident data will not be shared except as required by applicable law\n• Resident data will not be used for marketing or business development purposes\n\nData Storage and Cybersecurity Controls\n• Encryption in transit (TLS 1.2+) and at rest (AES-256)\n• Role-based access controls limiting data access to authorized personnel only\n• Multi-factor authentication required for all administrative accounts\n• Audit logging of all administrative data access\n• Regular third-party security assessments\n\nIncident Response\nIn the event of a confirmed or suspected data breach, EZ Park will notify the City within 24 hours of\ndetection. The notification will include a description of the incident, data potentially affected, immediate\ncontainment steps taken, and a remediation plan. EZ Park will comply fully with all applicable Utah breach\nnotification requirements. Page 19 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":20,"text":"EZ PARK LLC | Proposal: City of Vineyard Residential Parking Program Confidential\n\nPage 9 of 11\n\n6. IMPLEMENTATION PLAN\n\nImplementation Timeline\nEZ Park will complete full implementation within thirty (30) days of contract award. Our existing operational\npresence in Vineyard, including established vendor relationships, patrol infrastructure, and local staff\nfamiliarity, significantly reduces the time and complexity of standing up the program. The plan below is\nstructured to prioritize physical infrastructure first and resident-facing digital setup second, so that signage\nand enforcement zones are in place before residents are asked to register.\n\nDays Phase Activities\n\n1 - 5 Kickoff and Scope\n\nFinalization\n\nContract execution, project kickoff meeting with City staff,\nenforcement zone mapping, permit structure confirmed,\n\nCity administrator accounts provisioned\n\n6 - 12 Sign Installation and\n\nZone Setup\n\nPhysical signage installed in all designated neighborhoods,\n\nQR code permit registration signs posted, LPR patrol\nroutes established and tested, system configured to match\nzone boundaries\n13 - 18 Digital Onboarding Resident portal goes live, welcome letters and registration\ninstructions distributed to all households, City staff training\ncompleted on dashboard and reporting tools, FAQ\nmaterials posted\n\n19 - 24 Soft Launch LPR patrols begin with citation-only enforcement. No boots\nor tows during this phase. Residents receive citations\ndirecting them to register. Daily check-ins with City Code\n\nEnforcement to address questions and edge cases.\n25 - 28 Full Enforcement Progressive enforcement goes fully live. Boots authorized\nfor repeat violations. First monthly enforcement summary\nprepared and delivered to City contacts.\n29 - 30 Review and Handoff 30-day review meeting with City staff, registration and\nenforcement data reviewed, any system adjustments\nmade, ongoing reporting cadence confirmed\n\nTraining\n• City administrator training on the permit management dashboard, report generation, and\nenforcement record access\n• City Code Enforcement personnel briefing on enforcement protocols, override procedures, and\nincident communication channels\n• Written quick-reference guide and user documentation provided to all City staff\n\nResident Education and Public Outreach\n• Welcome letter explaining the digital permit system, how to register, and what to expect from\nenforcement\n• FAQ document addressing common resident questions about the program\n• QR code signage at all enforcement zone entry points linking to the self-registration portal\n• EZ Park resident support line and email available throughout the onboarding window Page 20 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":21,"text":"EZ PARK LLC | Proposal: City of Vineyard Residential Parking Program Confidential\n\nPage 10 of 11\nEZ Park is available to participate in City-sponsored community meetings or outreach sessions during the\nimplementation period.\n\nStaffing and Operational Readiness\nEZ Park has patrol drivers, enforcement equipment, LPR technology, and operational infrastructure\ncurrently deployed in the Vineyard area. There is no ramp-up period required for equipment procurement\nor route familiarization. We are ready to begin patrols within 48 hours of zone setup completion.\n\nPatrol coverage for the City of Vineyard program will be structured as follows:\n• Patrol frequency: 24/7 roving patrol unless otherwise specified in the contract\n• Staffing: a dedicated LPR patrol vehicle and trained driver assigned to Vineyard enforcement\nzones each patrol night\n• Response coverage: EZ Park personnel available by phone during all active patrol hours for City\n\nCode Enforcement inquiries and boot release requests\n• Patrol logs, including GPS-timestamped route data and LPR scan records, are retained in the\nsystem and accessible to City staff through the administrative dashboard\n\n7. COST PROPOSAL\n\nPricing Overview\nEZ Park operates on a transparent, flat-fee pricing model with no hidden charges, no revenue sharing on\nenforcement actions, and no upfront setup costs. All signage, system configuration, and initial resident\nonboarding are included in the monthly service fee.\nBecause the number of designated neighborhoods, addresses, and patrol frequency requirements will be\nfinalized in contract negotiations, EZ Park proposes to confirm final pricing upon scope confirmation. We\nwelcome the opportunity to present detailed pricing during the evaluation and interview phase.\n\nService City Cost Resident Cost\n\nDigital Permit Management Platform (setup +\nongoing)\n\nTBD upon scope\nconfirmation\n\nNo charge\n\nLPR Patrol Enforcement (nightly) TBD upon scope\nconfirmation\n\nNo charge\n\nCitation Issuance Included No charge\n\nBoot Installation No charge to City $75 per incident\n\nBoot Removal No charge to City Included in boot fee\n\nTowing (when authorized) No charge to City Tow company statutory\nrate per Utah law\n\nVehicle Storage (when towed) No charge to City Tow company statutory\nrate per Utah law\n\nAdministrative Fees None None\n\nAfter-Hours Fees (EZ Park) None None\nSystem Setup and Implementation No additional charge N/A Page 21 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":22,"text":"EZ PARK LLC | Proposal: City of Vineyard Residential Parking Program Confidential\n\nPage 11 of 11\n\nStaff and Resident Training No additional charge N/A\n\nPaid Visitor Parking (optional) No charge to City Per-session rate set by\n\nCity; revenue remitted to\n\nCity monthly\nEZ Park does not impose undisclosed fees. The schedule above represents the complete universe of\ncharges associated with this program. Towing and storage fees are charged by the independent licensed\ntow company and governed by Utah statutory limits. EZ Park receives no markup, commission, or financial\ninterest in towing activity.\n\nContract Term\nEZ Park acknowledges and accepts the proposed contract structure of a three-year initial term with\nautomatic annual renewals thereafter, subject to termination by either party with thirty days written notice.\nWe view the three-year initial term as appropriate for a program of this nature. Building resident familiarity\nwith the permit system and establishing consistent enforcement patterns takes time, and a multi-year\nstructure allows both parties to see the program through to its full potential rather than evaluating it on\nearly-stage metrics alone.\n\nCLOSING STATEMENT\nEZ Park LLC is uniquely positioned to serve the City of Vineyard in this program. We are not a national\ncompany with a remote account team. We are a Utah-based company with boots on the ground in\nVineyard today, serving your residents' neighbors and operating on your city's streets.\nOur enforcement philosophy was designed from the ground up to match exactly what the City has\ndescribed in this RFP: fair, transparent, resident-first enforcement that protects community quality of life\nwithout resorting to predatory towing. Parking management done right should be something residents\nbarely notice, because the system is working smoothly in the background.\nWe welcome the opportunity to meet with City staff, answer questions, and demonstrate our platform.\n\nThank you for considering EZ Park LLC.\n\nRespectfully submitted,\n\nCam Terry\n\nChief Executive Officer, EZ Park LLC\n(435) 231-3794 | Ezonboarding@ezparkllc.com | www.ezparkllc.com Page 22 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":23,"text":"Page 23 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":24,"text":"VINEYARD CITY, UTAH\n\nVineyard\n\nEconomic\n\nDevelopment\n\nPartnership\n\nA voluntary, no-cost, City-led\ninitiative that brings Vineyard City\ntogether with local businesses,\nproperty owners, and developers to\ngrow the local economy.\n\nWhat the Partnership does\n\n01 Supports Vineyard businesses\n\nHelps the businesses already here stay and expand.\n\n02 Attracts new employers and investment\n\nRecruits businesses and coordinates investment in Vineyard.\n\n03 Identifies commercial opportunities\n\nConnects businesses, developers, and property owners.\n\n04 Meets every quarter\n\nBusinesses and City staff review conditions and priorities.\n\n05 Shares information and resources\n\nBusiness resources and City priorities, with a VEDP online portal.\nVoluntary participation No dues or fees Led by Vineyard City Page 24 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":25,"text":"THE VEDP PORTAL\n\nVineyard\n\nEconomic\n\nDevelopment\n\nPortal\n\nA public online portal that gives\nbusinesses and investors outside\n\nVineyard the city's demographics,\nmarket data, and business\nresources in one place.\n\nVineyard at a glance\n\nA sample of the data the portal will show\n\n25.2\nmedian age\n\nResidents by age\n\nUnder 15 24.6%\n\n15 to 24 24.7%\n\n25 to 44 38.4%\n\n45 to 64 10.3%\n\n65 and over 2.1%\n\n16,092\n\nResidents, July\n\n2025\n$103,380\n\nMedian household\nincome\n\n50.5%\n\nAdults with a bachelor's\ndegree or higher\n\n4,723\n\nHouseholds\nSource: U.S. Census Bureau. Population is the July 1, 2025 estimate. Other figures are American\nCommunity Survey 2020 to 2024 five-year estimates. Page 25 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":26,"text":"VINEYARD CITY, UTAH\n\nRESOLUTION NO. 2026-41\n\nA RESOLUTION ESTABLISHING THE VINEYARD ECONOMIC DEVELOPMENT\n\nPARTNERSHIP AS A CITY-LED ECONOMIC DEVELOPMENT INITIATIVE\nWHEREAS, Vineyard City seeks to support existing businesses, attract new employers and investment,\nexpand commercial opportunities, and strengthen the City’s long-term economic and tax base;\nWHEREAS, ongoing communication among the City, local businesses, property owners, developers, and\nother stakeholders can help identify challenges, coordinate opportunities, and improve economic\ndevelopment outcomes;\nWHEREAS, the City Council desires to establish the Vineyard Economic Development Partnership\n(VEDP) as a voluntary, no-cost, City-led initiative for business engagement and economic development\ncoordination;\n\nNOW, THEREFORE, BE IT RESOLVED BY THE CITY COUNCIL OF VINEYARD CITY, UTAH:\nSection 1. Establishment. The Vineyard Economic Development Partnership (VEDP) is hereby established as\na City-led economic development initiative administered through existing City operations. The VEDP is not\nestablished as a separate legal entity, independent public body, advisory commission, or membership\norganization with governmental authority.\nSection 2. Purpose. The VEDP shall facilitate communication and collaboration among existing Vineyard\nbusinesses, prospective businesses, developers, property owners, and other economic development\nstakeholders. Its objectives include business retention and expansion, business recruitment, investment\ncoordination, identification of commercial opportunities, and the exchange of information regarding\neconomic conditions and City priorities.\nSection 3. Participation. Participation shall be voluntary and open to businesses operating in Vineyard and\nother stakeholders invited by City staff based on the initiative’s purpose. No membership dues or\nparticipation fees shall be charged. Participation does not confer endorsement by the City, preferred status,\ndecision-making authority, or any entitlement to public funding or incentives.\nSection 4. Administration. The VEDP shall be led and coordinated by the City’s Economic Development\nDirector, Stephen Proud, in coordination with the City Manager and other relevant City staff, including\nRedevelopment Agency staff as appropriate. The initiative shall operate using existing personnel and\nresources, subject to adopted budgets and applicable law. Staff may maintain a contact directory, distribute\nupdates, facilitate introductions, and coordinate outreach and recruitment activities.\nSection 5. Quarterly Engagement. The Economic Development Director, in coordination with relevant\nCity staff, shall organize a VEDP meeting once each calendar quarter for participating businesses and\nstakeholders to discuss business conditions, development activity, opportunities, concerns, and City\neconomic development priorities.\nMeetings shall be informational and collaborative and shall not exercise delegated governmental decision-\nmaking authority. Page 26 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":27,"text":"Section 6. Communications and Economic Development Portal. As part of the VEDP initiative, City staff,\nunder the coordination of the Economic Development Director, are authorized to develop and maintain a\nVEDP webpage or online economic development portal. The portal may promote Vineyard as a place to\ndo business and provide prospective and existing businesses and investors with relevant information,\nincluding City demographics, economic and market data, development opportunities, business resources,\nCity priorities, participation opportunities, and contact information. Staff shall coordinate with relevant\ndepartments to identify and present appropriate information. Any listing or use of business names or marks\nshall comply with applicable law and City communication standards. The portal shall distinguish existing\nVineyard businesses from businesses that have affirmatively elected to participate.\nSection 7. Funding and Administrative Support. The Vineyard Economic Development Partnership shall\noperate utilizing existing City resources, facilities, personnel, and available appropriations. The Economic\nDevelopment Director, in coordination with the City Manager and relevant City staff, is authorized to\nincur reasonable and necessary expenses associated with the administration, meetings, activities, outreach,\nand operations of the Partnership, subject to available budget appropriations and applicable City\npurchasing and financial policies. No separate appropriation or additional City Council approval shall be\nrequired for expenditures within existing authorized budgets and administrative spending authority.\nSection 8. Authority and Oversight. The VEDP shall not independently enter contracts, grant incentives,\napprove projects, or bind Vineyard City or its Redevelopment Agency. Any action requiring official\napproval shall remain subject to the appropriate governing body and applicable law. Staff shall provide the\n\nCity Council with periodic updates regarding the initiative.\nSection 9. Implementation and Effective Date. The City Manager is authorized to take reasonable\nadministrative steps to implement this resolution within existing appropriations. This resolution shall take\neffect immediately upon adoption.\n\nPASSED AND ADOPTED by the Vineyard City Council this day of , 2026.\n\nATTEST:\n___________________________________________ ___________________________________\n\nZack Stratton, Vineyard City Mayor Robin Bond, City Recorder\n\nVOTING YES NO ABSENT ABSTAIN\n\nMayor Zach Stratton (votes only in\ncase of a tie)\n\nCouncil Member Jacob Holdaway\n\nCouncil Member David Lauret\n\nCouncil Member Parker McCumber\n\nCouncil Member Chip Price\nCouncil Member Jacob Wood Page 27 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":28,"text":"Page 28 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":29,"text":"Page 1\n\nVINEYARD\n\nORDINANCE 2026-12\n\nAN ORDINANCE OF THE VINEYARD CITY COUNCIL AMENDING\n\nMUNICIPAL CODE SECTION 2.30.020, RENAMING AND REORGANIZING\n\nTHE ACTIVE TRANSPORTATION COMMISSION AS THE PEDESTRIAN\n\nSAFETY COMMISSION, REVISING ITS PURPOSE AND DUTIES,\n\nRECONSTITUTING ITS MEMBERSHIP, ESTABLISHING MONTHLY\n\nMEETINGS, AND PROVIDING FOR A SUNSET DATE\nWHEREAS, the Vineyard City Council, under the authority of Utah Code Title 10, Chapter\n\n3, desires to amend the Vineyard Municipal Code; and\nWHEREAS, Section 2.30.020 of the Vineyard Municipal Code presently establishes the\n\nActive Transportation Commission as an advisory commission of the City; and\nWHEREAS, the City Council desires to refocus the commission on the safety of people\ntraveling on, along, and across Vineyard streets and other public rights-of-way, including\npeople walking, crossing streets, using mobility devices, and using bicycles, electric\nbicycles, scooters, and other micromobility devices; and\nWHEREAS, the City Council finds that pedestrian safety is advanced through careful\nreview of street conditions, speed and traffic data, crossings, school routes, visibility, traffic\ncalming measures, education, and coordination with City staff and public safety partners;\nand\nWHEREAS, the City Council desires to retain the general commission structure established\nin Chapter 2.30 while reorganizing the commission, requiring new appointments,\nestablishing a monthly meeting schedule, and setting a sunset date for review of the\ncommission; and\n\nWHEREAS, the City Council finds that the amendments contained in this Ordinance\npromote the public health, safety, and welfare of Vineyard residents and visitors.\nNOW THEREFORE, be it ordained by the Council of the Vineyard, in the State of Utah,\nas follows:\n\nSECTION 1: AMENDMENT “2.30.020 Active Transportation Commission” of the\nVineyard Municipal Code is hereby amended and replaced in its entirety as follows:\n\nA M E N D M E N T\n\n2.30.020 Pedestrian Safety Commission\n\nA. GENERAL.\nThe Active Transportation Commission is hereby renamed, reorganized, and reconstituted as\nthe Pedestrian Safety Commission. The Commission shall serve as an advisory commission\nto the Mayor and City Council concerning the safety of people traveling on, along, and\nacross streets, intersections, sidewalks, trails, crossings, transit access points, and other\npublic rights-of-way within Vineyard.\n\nB. PEDESTRIAN SAFETY SCOPE.\nFor purposes of this Section, the Commission's pedestrian safety scope includes the safety of\npersons walking or running and persons using wheelchairs or other mobility devices, Page 29 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":30,"text":"Page 2\nbicycles, electric bicycles, scooters, skateboards, and other micromobility devices when\nthose persons interact with streets, intersections, crossings, sidewalks, shared-use paths,\ntrails, transit access points, or other public rights-of-way. This subsection defines the\nadvisory scope of the Commission only and does not alter any definition contained\nelsewhere in the Vineyard Municipal Code or Utah law.\n\nC. PURPOSES AND DUTIES.\nThe Pedestrian Safety Commission shall have the responsibilities provided in this Section\nand such other pedestrian safety related duties and priorities as may be established by the\n\nMayor and City Council:\n1. Advise and provide input on City policies, plans, projects, design standards, rules,\nregulations, and ordinances that materially affect pedestrian safety.\n2. Identify and evaluate pedestrian safety concerns, including unsafe crossings, school routes,\nintersections, transit access points, areas with limited visibility, locations with recurring\nconflicts among roadway users, and corridors where vehicle speeds create safety\nconcerns.\n3. Review available crash, incident, near-miss, speed, traffic-volume, pedestrian-count, and\nrelated safety information, and recommend appropriate traffic studies, speed studies,\npedestrian studies, or other data collection to better understand identified safety\nconcerns.\n4. Recommend traffic calming and safety improvements, including, when appropriate,\ncrosswalk improvements, signage, striping, lighting, signal timing, pedestrian refuge\nareas, curb extensions, raised crossings, speed feedback devices, sight-distance\nimprovements, and other measures intended to reduce risk to people traveling on or\nacross public streets.\n5. Develop, recommend, and participate in public education efforts concerning safe street\ncrossings, helmet use, visibility, bicycle and electric bicycle safety, scooter and\nmicromobility safety, driver awareness, school-route safety, and the safe sharing of\npublic rights-of-way.\n6. Provide a venue for public input regarding pedestrian safety and assist the City by\nidentifying locations, patterns, and conditions that may warrant additional review by City\nstaff or public safety personnel.\n7. Provide pedestrian-safety feedback on current and future transportation plans, capital\nprojects, street designs, development-related transportation improvements, and other\nprojects when requested by the Mayor, City Council, or City staff.\n8. Coordinate, as appropriate, with City departments, law enforcement, schools, homeowners\nassociations, Utah County, the Utah Department of Transportation, regional\ntransportation partners, and other public or private organizations on pedestrian safety\nmatters.\n9. Identify grants, partnerships, pilot programs, studies, technology, or other opportunities\nthat may assist the City in improving pedestrian safety or evaluating potential safety\nimprovements.\n10. Prepare an annual report identifying Commission activities, significant pedestrian safety\nconcerns, recommended priorities, and proposed educational or infrastructure initiatives\nfor consideration by the Mayor and City Council.\n11. Perform such other pedestrian safety related duties, studies, educational initiatives, and\nwork assignments as may be established from time to time by the Mayor and City\nCouncil. Page 30 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":31,"text":"Page 3\n\nD. MEMBERSHIP AND RECONSTITUTION.\n1. Except as expressly provided in this Section, the composition, appointment, terms,\nremoval, vacancies, and general procedures of the Commission shall be governed by\nSection 2.30.010 of the Vineyard Municipal Code. Consistent with the existing Active\nTransportation Commission structure, up to two (2) members may be appointed at-large\nand may reside anywhere in the State of Utah.\n2. Upon the effective date of this Ordinance, all current appointments to the Active\nTransportation Commission, including regular members, alternate members, and any ex-\nofficio member, shall automatically terminate and all seats shall be deemed vacant. The\nCommission shall thereafter be filled by appointment of the Mayor with the advice and\nconsent of the City Council in accordance with Chapter 2.30.\n3. Former members of the Active Transportation Commission are eligible to apply for\nappointment to the Pedestrian Safety Commission and may be appointed or reappointed\nin the same manner as any other applicant.\n4. No appointment to the Pedestrian Safety Commission shall continue beyond the sunset\ndate established in Subsection G unless the Commission is renewed or extended by\nordinance.\n\nE. MEETINGS.\nThe Pedestrian Safety Commission shall establish a regular meeting schedule providing for\none (1) regular meeting each month. A scheduled meeting may be canceled or rescheduled\nwhen there is insufficient business, due to a holiday, or for other reasonable circumstances,\nprovided that any meeting is noticed and conducted in accordance with the Utah Open and\n\nPublic Meetings Act and applicable City requirements.\n\nF. ADVISORY AUTHORITY AND COUNCIL PRIORITIES.\n1. The Commission is advisory only and shall have no authority to bind the City, expend City\nfunds, direct City employees, approve projects, or enforce City ordinances.\n2. The Mayor and City Council may, from time to time, establish additional goals, priorities,\nstudies, educational initiatives, or work assignments for the Commission that are\nconsistent with the pedestrian safety purpose established by this Section.\n\nG. SUNSET.\nThe Pedestrian Safety Commission shall remain in effect through December 31, 2027.\nUnless renewed or extended by ordinance adopted by the City Council before that date, the\nCommission shall sunset and cease to exist at 11:59 p.m. on December 31, 2027, and this\n\nSection 2.30.020 shall thereafter be of no further force or effect.\n\nSECTION 2: TRANSITION AND CONTINUITY.\nAll records, minutes, pending recommendations, studies, work products, and other official\nmaterials of the Active Transportation Commission shall be retained by the City and may be\ncontinued, reviewed, modified, or closed by the Pedestrian Safety Commission as appropriate.\nReferences in existing City records or administrative materials to the Active Transportation\nCommission shall be construed as references to the Pedestrian Safety Commission when\nconsistent with the purpose of this Ordinance.\n\nSECTION 3: REPEALER CLAUSE.\nAll ordinances, resolutions, or parts thereof that are in conflict with this Ordinance are hereby\nrepealed to the extent of such conflict. Page 31 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":32,"text":"Page 4\n\nSECTION 4: SEVERABILITY CLAUSE.\nShould any part or provision of this Ordinance be declared by a court of competent jurisdiction\nto be unconstitutional, invalid, or unenforceable, such decision shall not affect the validity of\nthe Ordinance as a whole or any part thereof other than the part so declared unconstitutional,\ninvalid, or unenforceable.\n\nSECTION 5: EFFECTIVE DATE.\nThis Ordinance shall be in full force and effect upon adoption and after the required approval\nand publication according to law.\n\nPASSED AND ADOPTED BY THE VINEYARD COUNCIL this day of\n, 2026.\n_________________________________________________________________________________________________\n\nAYE NAY ABSENT ABSTAIN\n\nJacob Holdaway\n\nDavid Lauret\n\nParker McCumber\n\nChip Price\n\nJacob Wood\n_________________________________________________________________________________________________\n\nPresiding Officer\n\nMayor, Vineyard\n\nAttest\nCity Recorder, Vineyard Page 32 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":33,"text":"VINEYARD CITY COUNCIL STAFF REPORT\n\nMeeting Date: October 13, 2026\nAgenda Item: Membership in the North Utah County Aquifer Council (NUCAC) (5 Minutes)\n\nDepartment: Public Works\n\nPresenter: Devan Peterson\n\nBackground/Discussion:\nThe North Utah County Aquifer Council (NUCAC) was established in 2017 to coordinate\nregional groundwater planning, aquifer recharge, and water resource management among\nmunicipalities in northern Utah County, which include Central Utah Water Conservancy District,\nAmerican Fork City, Pleasant Grove, Highland City, Alpine City, Lehi City, Lindon City, the\n\nCity of Cedar Hills, and Orem City.\nVineyard City's Drinking Water Master Plan identifies securing future water sources as a\nstrategic need to support the City's continued growth and long-term water supply reliability.\nRegional coordination and evaluation of additional water sources are key to addressing this need.\n\nNUCAC is currently undertaking a Regional Water Resource Master Plan to evaluate\ngroundwater resources, future water demands, aquifer recharge opportunities, and regional water\nsupply strategies. The planning effort is estimated at approximately $700,000, funded through\nNUCAC member dues and more than $500,000 in direct contributions from Central Utah Water\n\nConservancy District (CUWCD), which is also a NUCAC member.\nMembership would give Vineyard City a voice in regional water resource planning, access to\ntechnical studies, and opportunities to coordinate future groundwater development and water-\nsharing initiatives with neighboring municipalities. NUCAC serves in an advisory capacity and\ndoes not supersede the City's water rights or authority over its water infrastructure.\n\nFiscal Impact:\nThe initial membership cost is $12,000, covering 2025 and 2026, with approximately $6,000\nannually thereafter.\n\nFunds are approved in the FY 2026–2027 Water Enterprise Fund budget 51-5101-4301.\n\nRecommendation:\nStaff recommends approval of the resolution authorizing Vineyard City's membership in\n\nNUCAC and execution of the associated Interlocal Agreement.\n\nSample Motion:\n\"I move to approve Resolution No. 2026-42, authorizing Vineyard City to become a member of\nthe North Utah County Aquifer Council and execute the associated Interlocal Agreement.\n\nAttachments:\n\n1. Final Signed Agreement Creating NUCAC conformed updated 06022025\n\n2. 8.4 RES 2026-42 Resolution by Cities Joining NUCAC - Updated FINAL 2026\n3. 8.4 Signature Page - City of Vineyard Page 33 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":34,"text":"Page 34 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":35,"text":"INTERLOCAL AGREEMENT\n\nCREATING THE NORTH UTAH COUNTY AQUIFER COUNCIL\nTIDS INTERLOCAL AGREEMENT (\"Agreement\") is made and entered into as of the z e-...,.-μ\nday of Fi;:.grz.v,a..,,a'f , 2017 (the \"Effective Date\"), under the authority of the Utah Interlocal\nCooperation Act, Title 11, Chapter 13, Utah Code Ann. 1953, as amended (the \"Act\"), and specifically\nSection 11-13-203 of the Act, by and among Central Utah Water Conservancy District, a water\nconservancy district organized under the laws of the State of Utah, and the following Utah municipal\ncorporations situated within northern Utah County: Pleasant Grove City, American Fork City, Highland\nCity, Alpine City, Lehi City, and Saratoga Springs City (all being hereinafter sometimes referred to herein\ncollectively as the \"Members,\" and individually as a \"Member\").\n\nWITNESSETH\nWHEREAS, it is generally recognized and agreed among the Members that the limited surface\nwater and groundwater resources in north Utah County are hydrologically connected and directly\ninterrelated; and\nWHEREAS, each of the Members, under the authority of their respective water rights, own\nsurface water diversion structures, groundwater wells and other facilities from and through which they\neach draw water from the common surface and groundwater sources of supply available in north Utah\n\nCounty for diversion and use within their respective service areas; and\nWHEREAS, the Members each recognize the need to plan and work together in preserving,\nprotecting and where possible enhancing the critical water resources they share in common as each of the\nMembers continues to develop their respective water systems in satisfaction of the demands of continuing\npopulation growth in north Utah County; and\nWHEREAS, under the auspices of a now dissolved interlocal agency previously formed among\nthe Members, an Aquifer Storage and Recovery Feasibility Study (the \"ASR Study\") was commissioned\nand completed which investigated the potential for recharging the ground water in north Utah County in\naccordance with the authority of the Utah Groundwater Recharge and Recovery Act; and\n\nWHEREAS, the ASR Study recommended several potential Aquifer Storage and Recovery\n(\"ASR\") projects that would benefit the groundwater resources in north Utah County; and\nWHEREAS, the Members have contributed to the preparation of and have reviewed various\nplanning studies and documents commissioned for the purpose of investigating the surface and\ngroundwater resources in north Utah County, including, but not limited to, the following:\n(1) Conjunctive Management of Surface and Groundwater in Utah. State of Utah Natural\n\nResources, Division of Water Resources, July, 2005.\n{O 108698 1-1}\n3 . Y. GO I . rv vc... . A O . bo l Page 35 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":36,"text":"(2) Hydrology of Northern Utah Valley, Utah County, Utah, 1975 - 2005, Scientific\n\nReport 2008-5197, U.S. Department of Interior, U.S . Geological Survey.\n(3) Three Dimensional Numerical Model of Groundwater Flow in Northern Utah Valley,\nUtah County, Utah. Scientific Investigation Report 2008-5049. U.S. Department of the Interior,\n\nU.S. Geological Survey.\n(4) North Utah County Groundwater Recharge Study, Highland City, Department of\n\nPublic Works, January, 2009\n(5) Aquifer Storage and Recovery (ASR) Feasibility Study, North Utah County Aquifer\n\nAssociation, Prepared by Hansen, Allen, & Luce, Inc., August, 2012.\n(6) Utah Lake Basin Planning for the Future. June, 2014. Utah State Water Plan. Utah\n\nDivision of Water Resources.\nWHEREAS, it is the intent of the Members that all Groundwater Projects be developed by one or\nmore Members under their individual water rights through projects funded and completed by the\nindividual Member or Members pursuing the project; and\nWHEREAS, given the hydro logic connectivity of the surface and groundwater sources of supply\nin north Utah County, the common interests of all the Members in the continued diversion and use of the\nsame groundwater supply, and the inevitability of increased withdrawals by the Members from these\nsources, the Members have determined it to be in the best interest of each of the Members individually,\nand in their mutual common interest and the interests of the citizens whom they serve, that an interlocal\ncouncil be established among the Members, the purpose and function of which is to coordinate and\nfacilitate the on-going planning and development of groundwater resources in north Utah County and\nMember Groundwater Projects as they develop, as more particularly set forth herein;\nNOW, THEREFORE, in consideration of the mutual covenants and agreements of the Members\ncontained herein, and for other good and valuable consideration, the receipt and sufficiency of which are\nhereby acknowledged, the undersigned Members hereby agree as follows:\n\nARTICLE I\n\nCREATION AND NAME\nPursuant to and in conformance with the requirements of Section 11-13-202 of the Act, the\nMembers hereby create the NORTH UTAH COUNTY AQUIFER COUNCIL (the \"Council\"), acting\njointly and cooperatively in the fulfillment of its purposes, and having only those powers, enumerated\nherein and not as an interlocal entity as defined in the Act.\n{ 0108698 1-1 }\n\nARTICLE II\n\nMEMBERSHIP\n2 Page 36 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":37,"text":"2.1. Members. Each Member signatory to this Agreement, and each additional public agency,\nas defined in the Act, which is accepted for membership as provided in this Agreement and which shall\nhereafter sign this Agreement shall be a Member of the Council and be entitled to all rights, privileges\nand obligations of membership as provided herein.\n\n2.2. Obligations of Membership. Each Member of the Council shall be obligated:\n2.2.1. to duly appoint a representative to serve on the governing Board of the Council as\nprovided in Section 4.1 herein, and to otherwise remain an active participant in the Council;\n2.2.2. to timely pay when due annual and special assessments as shall be duly levied, by\nthe Council Administrator as designated in Section 4.2 herein, from year-to-year to fund the Council's\npurposes, powers and functions as provided on this Agreement; and\n2.2.3. to timely review, comment on and respond to matters submitted by the Board to\nthe governing boards of the Council Members.\n2.3. Donation of In-kind Resources. Members, at their sole discretion, may contribute\nresources via in-kind materials, equipment, facilities or services which benefit the Council; however, any\nsuch contribution shall not be accepted in lieu, partially or in full, of payment of assessments duly levied\nby the Administrative Agency as provided herein in Section 4.2.2(a) herein, unless otherwise approved by\nthe Board.\n\n2.4. Suspension and Termination of Membership. Membership in the Council may be\nterminated in conformance with the following:\n\n2.4.1. Voluntary Withdrawal.\n(a) Any Member which desires to withdraw from the Council may do so by its\ngoverning body adopting a resolution of withdrawal to be submitted to the Council's governing board as\ndefined herein.\n(b) The withdrawal and termination of membership shall be effective as of the\nend of the Council's then current fiscal year. Withdrawal of a Member does not relieve the Member's\nobligation to pay its annual financial obligations to the Council for the then current fiscal year as set forth\nin Article V, or its share of obligations and liabilities incurred prior to withdrawal.\n(c) A Member terminating its membership shall have no interest in the assets of\nthe Council unless it is a Member at the time of dissolution of the Council.\n(d) A Member who withdraws its membership shall have no further obligations\nto the Council and the Council shall have no further obligations to the withdrawn Member, except as\n{O I 08698 1-1 }\n3 Page 37 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":38,"text":"otherwise expressly provided for herein. The fact that a Member has previously withdrawn its\nmembership or its membership has been cancelled shall not prohibit said Member from rejoining the\n\nCouncil pursuant to the authority of Section 2.5 herein.\n2.4.2. Failure to Pay Assessments. The failure by a Member to pay assessments when\ndue shall result in the suspension of that Member's membership for a period of sixty (60) days unless\npayment is sooner made in full. The failure to pay the past due assessment by the end of the 60-day\nsuspension period shall result in the termination of membership effective immediately as of said date.\nThe Member shall not be liable for the unpaid assessment that resulted in the termination of its\nmembership.\n\n2.4.3. Agreements Not Affected. Each Member of the Council acknowledges and\nagrees that the withdrawal of any Member from this Agreement pursuant to Section 2.2 shall not\nadversely affect this Agreement nor any other contractual relationship between the withdrawing Member\nand any other Member under this Agreement.\n2.5. Subsequent Membership. Prospective Members who do not initially approve and become\nsignatories to this Agreement, or who have withdrawn and wish to reapply, or who are newly created\nentities may join and become signatories to this Agreement upon approval of, and pursuant to conditions\nduly established by the Board, on a case-by-case basis.\n\nARTICLE III\n\nPURPOSES, POWERS AND FUNCTIONS OF THE COUNCIL\n3.1. Purposes, Powers and Functions. The purposes, powers and functions of the Council\nshall be as follows:\n3.1.1 . Study and Evaluate Groundwater Resources. The Council shall have the power to\nstudy, review, evaluate and analyze all aspects of surface and groundwater resources in north Utah\n\nCounty for the purpose of:\n(a) accumulating and disseminating to Members and other public agencies\ninformation pertaining to: (i) the hydrogeologic interactions between surface and groundwater resources;\n(ii) resource sustainability trends; (iii) the protection and optimal utilization of surface and groundwater\nresources; and (iv) the potential and methodology for recharging groundwater aquifers; and\n(b) establishing general surface and groundwater protection and development\ncriteria that can be considered by Members and other public agencies having jurisdiction over the north\n\nUtah County surface and groundwater resources; and\n(c) providing for public input, participation, and education on issues relating to\nthe use, protection, and development of surface and groundwater resources in north Utah County.\n\n3.1.2. Review and Coordination of Specific Groundwater Projects; Procedure.\n{0108698 l-l}\n4 Page 38 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":39,"text":"(a) For the purposes of this Section, the term \"Groundwater Project(s)\"shall be\ndefined to mean: (i) the design, drilling, testing and operation of wells from which groundwater is to be\nproduced and diverted from known groundwater aquifers within north Utah County; (ii) the design,\ndrilling, testing and operation of ASR projects through which water is to be injected and stored within and\nwithdrawn from groundwater aquifers within north Utah County; and (iii) the design, testing and\noperation of other surface and/or groundwater facilities; any of which may have the potential to either\nadversely impact or contribute to and assist in the recovery of groundwater resources in north Utah\n\nCounty.\n(b) Each Member proposing to initiate a Groundwater Project (\"Sponsoring\nMember\"), shall, no later than ten (10) working days prior to the next meeting of the Board, first submit\nto the Council, and to each of the other Members, a written detailed description of the Groundwater\nProject, together with the engineering, hydrologic and other technical data and materials generated and\nutilized by the Sponsoring Member as its basis for proceeding with the Groundwater Project (collectively,\nthe \"Project Proposal\").\n(c) At the meeting at which the Project Proposal is formally presented to the\nBoard, the Sponsoring Member shall present a detailed explanation of the proposed project and answer\nquestions.\n(d) Each of the other Members shall have 90 days from the date of the Board\nmeeting at which the Project Proposal is first presented (the \"Project Review Period\"), to review and\nconsider the Project Proposal and determine whether to join as a co-sponsor of the Groundwater Project.\nA decision by a Member to participate as a co-sponsor of a Groundwater Project shall be submitted in\nwriting to the Council and the Sponsoring Member prior to the end of the Project Review Period, unless\nthe time is extended by the Board.\n(e) The Board shall review and evaluate the water rights and water right change\napplications, recharge applications, recovery applications, and other applications and submittals as may\nbe required of the Sponsoring Member by other State, federal or local entities having jurisdiction over the\nGroundwater Project, for the purpose of identifying, addressing and assisting in the resolution of potential\nconflicts among the Members as a result of the Groundwater Project. Notwithstanding the foregoing, it is\nunderstood and agreed that the Council shall have no authority to veto or otherwise alter any Groundwater\nProject, and any Member may, in its discretion, protest any water right change application, or other\napplication as provided by law.\n( f) If there is more than one sponsor of an Groundwater Project, the co-sponsors\nshall, by separate interlocal agreement among them, set forth the terms and conditions pursuant to which\nthe Groundwater Project shall be planned, designed, funded, developed, administered, constructed,\nowned, operated, accounted for, maintained, repaired, renewed and replaced. If there are no co-sponsors\nfor an Groundwater Project, the Sponsoring Member shall, at its sole expense, plan, design, fund,\ndevelop, administer, construct, own, operate, account for maintain, repair, renew and replace the\n{01086981-1 }\n5 Page 39 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":40,"text":"Groundwater Project.\n(g) In the case of either a single sponsored or co-sponsored Groundwater Project,\nthe Sponsoring Member(s) of the Groundwater Project shall provide to the Council periodic updates and\nreports with respect to all aspects of the Groundwater Project during the course of its design, construction,\nand operation. The Council shall coordinate, review and report on all aspects of each Groundwater\nProject to the other Members of the Council in order to foster cooperation and promote maximum\nutilization of the groundwater resources among the Members, and to assist in the prevention and\nresolution of disputes among the Members pertaining to Groundwater Projects within north Utah County.\n3 .1.3. Advisory Resource to Members. The Council shall serve as an advisory body to\nthe Members' governing bodies concerning proposed and ongoing Groundwater Projects and other\nprojects involving groundwater sources of supply in north Utah County. Actions taken by the Board are\nnot binding on a Member unless ratified by that Member's governing body.\n3 .1.4. General Powers. The Council, in general, shall have the power to plan, oversee,\ncoordinate, review, advise, assist, share information and foster cooperation among the Members in the\npromotion and implementation of Member Groundwater Projects, in an effort to facilitate and coordinate\naquifer recharge and storage within north Utah County, while minimizing the negative impacts of\nexcessive groundwater withdrawals by the Members from the common aquifers as development occurs.\nThe Council shall not have the power to tax or assess and collect fees or spend more than the assessments\npaid by its Members as provided herein; however, the Council shall have the power to apply for, receive\nand administer grants from federal, state and/or private sources for use in fulfilling its purposes, powers\nand functions.\n\n3.2. No Superseding Authority. The Members hereby acknowledge and agree that the\nCouncil shall have no authority to supplant or otherwise interfere with any right, power or authority\nbelonging to any individual Member under the Utah Constitution, state law, or any county or municipal\nordinance, or by virtue of any other power or powers specifically given to them; nor does the Council\nhave superseding authority over other government entities and jurisdictions. The Council shall likewise\nhave no authority to require any alteration to the duly adopted plans or decisions of any Member or other\nagency or jurisdiction; however, the Board may, in the common interest of the Council, recommend\nchanges to the duly adopted plans or decisions of any Member, agency or jurisdiction, and each Member\nagrees, in good faith, to duly consider any such recommendation made.\n\n4.1. Governing Board.\n\nARTICLE IV\n\nGOVERNANCE\n4.1.1. Membership and Appointment. Pursuant to Section 11-1 3-207 of the Act, there is\nhereby created a governing board of the Council (the \"Board\"), which shall consist of one representative\nfrom each Member duly appointed by the governing body of the Member. To serve on the Board, the\n{0 108698 1- l }\n6 Page 40 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":41,"text":"Member representative shall hold public office or be employed with the Member and have responsibility\nfor the Member' s infrastructure related to the diversion and use of water and the development and\nmanagement of the Member's water supply. Due to the technical nature of the matters to be considered\nby the Board, it is recommended that member representatives holding public office have some technical\nunderstanding and/or background with respect to water, water rights and related issues. Each Member\nshall also appoint an alternate Member representative to serve on the Board in the absence of the regular\n\nBoard member.\n\n4.1.2. Board Terms. Board members shall serve until replaced by another\nrepresentative appointed by the Member.\n4.1.3. Compensation. Board members shall serve without compensation and shall have\nall Council-related expenses paid by their appointing Member.\n4.1.4. Leadership. The Board shall have a Chair and Vice-Chair elected by and from\nthe members of the Board, whose term shall be for a period of one year. Board members can be reelected\non an annual basis. In the absence of the Board chair, the responsibility for conducting Board meetings or\nsigning documents shall vest in the Vice-chair.\n\n4.1.5 . Regular Meetings. The Board should hold at least two regularly scheduled\nmeetings within each fiscal year to accomplish the objectives of the Council. The meeting date, time, and\nagenda will be established by the Chair or by a majority vote of the Board.\n4.1.6. Quorum; Voting. The attendance of a majority of the members of the Board at a\nmeeting of the Board shall constitute a quorum for the transaction of the business of the Board. Each\nBoard member shall be entitled to vote on all matters brought before the Board for a decision. A majority\nof the total votes cast by Board members at a meeting at which a quorum is present, shall constitute the\napproving action of the Board.\n4.1.7. Minutes. The Board shall cause to be kept minutes of all meetings of the Board.\nAs soon as possible after each meeting, a draft copy of the minutes shall be forwarded to the Board.\n4.1 .8 Notice of Meetings. Notice to Board members shall be sufficient if delivered in\nwriting or by e-mail one week in advance, at the address or e-mail address which shall be required to be\nprovided by each Board member to the Council.\n\n4.1.9 Requests for Information. The Board shall see that all of its Members are\ninformed regarding all activities of the Council and, accordingly, shall cause a copy of all materials to be\nconsidered at a meeting by the Board to be delivered one week in advance of the meeting to the individual\nBoard members and to such other persons as the Member may request in writing, in such manner as it\ndeems appropriate, including meeting agendas and minutes of past meetings.\n\n4.1.10 Board Powers and Duties. The Board shall have the following powers and\n{0 108698 1-1}\n7 Page 41 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":42,"text":"duties: (i) the power to elect the Chair and Vice Chair of the Board; (ii) the power to recommend\nproposed changes to this Agreement that must be approved by the Members' governing bodies; (iii) the\npower to adopt budgets, work plans, amendment of budgets or the allocation or reallocation of budgeted\namounts between budget categories; (iv) the power to establish rules and guidelines governing its own\nconduct; and (v) the power to govern, administer and implement directly or indirectly the powers,\npurposes and functions for which the Council was created.\n4.1.11 Records. The records of the Council shall be made available to the governing\nbody and/or legal counsel of each Member upon written request.\n\n4.1.12. Annual Work Plan. The Board shall establish an Annual Work Plan (\"Work\nPlan\"), which shall serve as a guideline for the Council's activities and the basis for the levy of\nassessments by CUWCD pursuant to Section 4.2.2(a) for the upcoming fiscal year. The Work Plan will\nalso identify long-range plans and objectives to be used for directing the efforts of the Council and as the\nbasis of estimating future assessment obligations of the Members. The Board shall consider related\nstudies performed by the public or private entities in its review of the Work Plan. The Board will approve\nthe Work Plan by March 15 of each year for the ensuing fiscal year beginning on July 1, and submit\ncopies to each Member.\n\n4.2. Council Administrator.\n4.2.1. Designated Council Administrator. Pursuant to Section 11-13-207 of the Act, the\nCentral Utah Water Conservancy District (\"CUWCD\"), is hereby designated and authorized to act as the\n\nCouncil Administrator.\n4.2.2. Powers and Duties. The Council hereby delegates to CUWCD, in its capacity as\n\nCouncil Administrator, the following powers and duties:\n(a) To levy assessments against the Members for the purpose of generating\nrevenues sufficient to accomplish the purposes of the Council, which revenue shall be collected and\nseparately accounted for by CUWCD on its books and financial records;\n(b) To apply for, receive and administer federal, state and local grants, and other\nprivate grants and donations from private persons and organizations to be utilized in accomplishing the\npurposes and powers of the Council;\n(c) To disperse funds and incur such financial obligations in behalf of the\nCouncil as shall be determined by the Board to be in the best interest of the Council and its Members; and\n(d) Within the limits of revenues received, to employ agents and consultants, and\nto make and enter into contracts in behalf of the Council, including, without limitation, contracts with the\nfederal government; the State of Utah; any county, municipality, local district or other governmental\nagency; any private foundation or organization; any business or other entity; and/or any individual; which\n{01086981-1}\n8 Page 42 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":43,"text":"are deemed by the Board to be in furtherance of the purpose and powers of the Council, as approved by\nthe Board in its sole discretion,\n4.2.3. Obligations Inure to the Council, not its Members. No obligation, debt and/or\nliability of the CUWCD acting in behalf of the Council shall constitute an obligation, debt or liability of\nany of the Council's individual Members.\n\n4.2.4. Procedures.\n(a) Procurement Procedures. All services and materials procured by CUWCD in\nbehalf of the Council shall be procured pursuant to the rules, regulations and policies of CUWCD in\nconformance with applicable State law.\n(b) Financial Procedures. The financial affairs of CUWCD, acting in its\ncapacity as the Council Administrator, shall be conducted in compliance with the provisions of Utah Code\nAnn. § 17B-1-601, Fiscal Procedures for Local Districts, and generally accepted accounting principles for\ngovernmental entities. CUWCD shall promulgate appropriate policies for the accounting, methods of\nmaintaining accounts, the payment of obligations, the preparation of annual budgets, and all other\nfinancial affairs with respect to its responsibilities as the Council Administrator.\n\n4.2.5. Financial Records; Fiscal Year.\n(a) Financial Records. CUWCD shall keep and maintain, or cause to be kept\nand maintained, adequate and correct financial records, including accounts of its assets, liabilities,\nreceipts, and disbursements pertaining to its responsibilities as Council Administrator. Financial reports\nshall be provided to the Members at least twice a year. All financial records which are maintained by the\nDistrict in its capacity as the Council Administrator shall be open during all reasonable business hours for\ninspection by duly authorized representatives of each Member and shall be deemed to be public records\nunder applicable Utah law.\n(b) Fiscal Year. The Council shall operate on, and all funds of the Council shall\nbe accounted for, on a fiscal year basis, commencing on July 1 and ending on June 30 of each year.\n\n4.2.6. Member Approval of Assessments; Subject to Availability of Funds.\n(a) All assessments levied by CUWCD against each Member shall be subject to\napproval by the governing body of each Member as a condition to its obligation of payment, subject to the\nprovisions of Section 2.3 herein.\n(b) The obligation of any Member to pay assessments or otherwise contribute to\nthe funding of the Council as provided herein is subject to the availability and annual appropriation of\nfunds by that Member's governing body. Each Member nevertheless agrees to act in good faith in\nfulfilling its funding obligations hereunder.\n{O 108698 l-l}\n9 Page 43 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":44,"text":"ARTICLEV\n\nLIABILITIES AND OBLIGATIONS\n5.1 . Governmental Immunity. In entering into this Agreement, the Members do not waive,\nand are not waiving, any immunity provided to the Members or their officials, employees, or agents by\nthe Utah Governmental Immunity Act, Title 63G, Chapter 7, Utah Code, or by other law.\n5.2 Waiver of Obligations. This Agreement shall not relieve any Member of any obligation\nor responsibility imposed upon it by law.\n5.3 Obligations Special and Limited. The obligations entered into by each Member pursuant\nto this Agreement are special limited obligations of each Member, and nothing herein shall be construed\nor give rise to a general obligation or liability of any Member or a charge against its general credit or\ntaxing powers.\n\nARTICLE VI\n\nTERM\nThe term of this Agreement shall begin on the Effective Date and be perpetual unless this\nAgreement is sooner terminated by dissolution pursuant to the provisions of Section 8 .1 herein, or by\noperation of law.\n\nARTICLE VII\n\nDISSOLUTION OF THE COUNCIL\n7.1 Dissolution of the Council by Vote. The Council may be dissolved at any time pursuant\nto a resolution of dissolution submitted by the governing bodies of two-thirds (2/3) of the then current\n\nMembers.\n7.2 Powers of Board Upon Dissolution. The Board is vested with all powers necessary for\nthe purpose of winding up and dissolving the business affairs of the Council consistent with and subject to\nthe limits of this Agreement.\n7.3. Division of Assets. Upon dissolution and after payment in full of all outstanding Council\nobligations, the Board shall equitably disburse the assets of the Council, if any, to the then current\nMembers. After deducting costs, any assets jointly shared shall be disbursed pro rata among the Members,\nunless otherwise agreed by the Members.\n\nARTICLE VIII\n\nMISCELLANEOUS PROVISIONS\n8 .1. Complete Agreement. The foregoing constitutes the full and complete Agreement of the\n{O 108698 l-l }\n10 Page 44 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":45,"text":"Members with respect to the subject matter hereof. There are no oral understandings or agreements with\nrespect thereto which are not set forth in writing herein.\n8.2. Binding Effect. This Agreement shall bind the Members and their respective successors-\nin-interest.\n8.3. Amendment. This Agreement may be amended at any time by the written approval of\ntwo-thirds (2/3) of the then current Members signatory to it.\n8.4. Prohibition Against Assignment. No Member may assign any right, claim, or interest it\nmay have under this Agreement, and no creditor, assignee, or third party beneficiary of any Member shall\nhave any right, claim, or title to any asset of the Council.\n8.5. Severability Clause. In the event that any article, provision, clause, or other part of this\nAgreement should be held invalid or unenforceable by a court of competent jurisdiction, such invalidity\nor unenforceability will not affect the validity or enforceability with respect to other articles, clauses,\napplications, or occurrences, and this Agreement is expressly declared to be severable.\n8.6. Captions. The captions to the various Sections of this Agreement are for convenience\nand ease of reference only and do not define, limit, augment, or describe the scope, content, or intent of\nthis Agreement or any part or parts of this Agreement.\n8.7. Breach of Agreement. The failure of a Member to substantially comply with the material\nterms and conditions of this Agreement shall constitute a breach of this Agreement. A Member shall have\nthirty (30) days after receipt of written notice to correct the conditions specified in the notice, or if the\ncorrections cannot be made within the thirty (30) day period, within a reasonable time if corrective action\nis commenced within ten (10) days after receipt of the notice. After notice, if corrective action is not\ntaken, the Board may take appropriate action including revocation of the breaching Member's\nmembership.\n8.8. Counterparts. This Agreement may be executed in counterparts, each of which shall be\ndeemed an original, but all of which shall constitute one and the same instrument.\n8.9. Warranty of Authority. The individuals executing this Agreement on behalf of the\nMembers hereby warrant that they have the requisite authority to execute this Agreement on behalf of the\nrespective Members and that the respective Members have agreed to be and are bound hereby.\n\nARTICLE IX\n\nATTORNEY APPROVAL; RECORDS FILING\n9 .1. Attorney Approval. This Agreement shall be submitted to an authorized attorney for\neach Member for approval as to form in accordance with Utah Code Ann. Section 11-13-202.5(3).\n9.2 . Records Filing. A copy of this duly executed Agreement shall be placed on file in the\n{O 108698 1-1}\n11 Page 45 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":46,"text":"Page 46 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":47,"text":"SIGNATURE PAGE\n\nINTERLOCAL AGREEMENT\n\nCREATING THE NORTH UTAH COUNTY AQUIFER COUNCIL\n\nIN WITNESS WHEREOF, the undersigned Member has executed this Agreement upon an\napproving resolution duly and lawfully passed to become effective on the Effective Date first set forth\nabove.\n~~ Its:\n\nDate: ~ ( · d 8\"J?(-::f\n\nAPPROVAL AS TO FORM:\n\nBy ~~,ffl.~ Attorney\n{01063449-1}\n13 Page 47 of