n\nDate:_~ /;)- _ --~ { q_ -i_ ~ _\n\nAPPROVAL AS TO FORM:\n\nBy ~ p'.~ mey\n{0108698 1-1}\n17 Page 52 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":53,"text":"{01063598-1}\n\nVINEYARD CITY\n\nRESOLUTION NO. 2026-42\n\nA RESOLUTION AUTHORIZING BECOMING A MEMBER OF\n\nTHAT CERTAIN INTERLOCAL AGREEMENT\n\nCREATING THE NORTH UTAH COUNTY AQUIFER COUNCIL,\n\nACCORDING TO THE TERMS THEREOF, AND RELATED MATTERS\nWHEREAS, Vineyard City, is a political subdivision created under the Municipal Code of the\nState of Utah for the purpose, among other things, of constructing water works and securing, providing\nand protecting a municipal water supply for its citizens, including extraterritorial jurisdiction with respect\nto the same; and\nWHEREAS, the City recognizes the critical inter-relationship between the groundwater and\nsurface water sources of supply in northern Utah County, and has a considerable interest in the\nmanagement, protection and conjunctive development of these water resources among those holding and\nowning rights in and to the use of the water developed from these sources of water supply; and\nWHEREAS , by Interlocal Agreement dated December 9, 2009 (the “Association Interlocal\nAgreement”), the District and certain northern Utah County cities, including Pleasant Grove City,\nAmerican Fork City, Highland City, Alpine City, Lehi City, and Saratoga Springs City (the “Association\nCities”), created the North Utah County Aquifer Association (the “Association”), for the purpose of\nperforming a feasibility study as to the potential for recharging the groundwater in northern Utah County\nin accordance with the Utah Groundwater Recharge and Recovery Act; and\nWHEREAS , the feasibility study having been completed in May, 2012, the District and the\nAssociation Cities, as the members of the Association, unanimously determined it to be in their collective\nbest interest to terminate the Association Interlocal Cooperation agreement and dissolve the Association;\nand\nWHEREAS , certain northern Utah County cities, including Pleasant Grove, American Fork City,\nHighland City, Alpine City, Lehi City (the “Council Cities”) and the District entered into an Interlocal\nAgreement (the “North Utah County Aquifer Council Interlocal Agreement”), dated February 28 th , 2017,\na copy of which is attached hereto, the purpose of which is to create, in place of the Association, an\ninterlocal council (the “North Utah County Aquifer Council”), as a non-legal entity, the purpose and\nfunction of which is to study, encourage, review, coordinate, and assist in facilitating the ongoing\nplanning, funding, and development of groundwater resources by its members, including possible\nrecharge and recovery projects, in northern Utah County; it being the express purpose and intent of the\nDistrict and the Council Cities, however, that all such projects be financed, designed, constructed,\noperated and maintained by the Council Cities and/or the District, individually or collectively, as the case\nmay be, pursuant to separate interlocal agreements related to each such project; and\nWHEREAS, paragraph 2.5 of the Interlocal Agreement provide for subsequent Membership of\nprospective members in the North Utah County Aquifer Council and become signatories to the Interlocal\nAgreement, pursuant to conditions duly established by the Board, on a case by case basis, which has\noccurred in a Council meeting in August 2026. Page 53 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":54,"text":"{01063598-1}\n\nNOW THERFORE, BE IT RESOLVED as follows:\n1. ___________________________ is hereby authorized to become a member of the North Utah\nCounty Aquifer Council, to participate on its governing board, and to execute the North Utah County\nAquifer Council Interlocal Agreement creating and governing for the same, in accordance with the terms\nthereof.\n2. ________________ is hereby appointed to serve as the Member representative on the Council\non behalf of the City, with _______________, to serve as the alternate Member representative.\n\n3. This Resolution shall be effective immediately upon execution hereof.\n\nPASSED AND APPROVED this ________ day of _____________, 2026.\n___________________________________________\n\nBy Mayor Zack Stratton\n\nCERTIFICATION\nI hereby certify that the foregoing is a true and accurate copy of a resolution adopted by the City\n\nCouncil of Vineyard on ________________, 2026.\n___________________________________________\n\nBy Robin Bond, City Recorder\n\nVOTING YES NO ABSENT ABSTAIN\n\nMayor Zach Stratton (votes only in\ncase of a tie)\n\nCouncil Member Jacob Holdaway\n\nCouncil Member David Lauret\n\nCouncil Member Parker\n\nMcCumber\n\nCouncil Member Chip Price\nCouncil Member Jacob Wood Page 54 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":55,"text":"{01086981-1}\n\nSIGNATURE PAGE\n\nINTERLOCAL AGREEMENT\n\nCREATING THE NORTH UTAH COUNTY AQUIFER COUNCIL\n\nSubsequent Membership to Council\n\nIN WITNESS WHEREOF , the undersigned Member has executed this Agreement upon an\napproving resolution duly and lawfully passed to become effective on the Effective Date first set forth\nabove.\n\nCITY OF VINEYARD\n\nBy_____________________________________\n\nIts:\n\nDate:____________________________\n\nAPPROVAL AS TO FORM:\n\nBy _____________________________________\n\nAttorney\n\nVOTING YES NO ABSENT ABSTAIN\n\nMayor Zach Stratton (votes only in\ncase of a tie)\n\nCouncil Member Jacob Holdaway\n\nCouncil Member David Lauret\n\nCouncil Member Parker McCumber\n\nCouncil Member Chip Price\n\nCouncil Member Jacob Wood\n\nResolution # ________________________ Date Approved __________________\n__________________________________\nCity Recorder Page 55 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":56,"text":"RESOLUTION NO. 2026-36\n\nA RESOLUTION OF THE VINEYARD CITY COUNCIL OPPOSING THE GENEVA RETENTION POND\n\nEXCHANGE AND REQUESTING A PUBLIC MANAGEMENT PLAN\nWHEREAS, Utah owns approximately 61.39 acres of former Geneva Steel retention-pond property on\nVineyard’s Utah Lake shoreline (the “Property”), and the Division of Forestry, Fire and State Lands\n(“FFSL”) proposes to lease that public land for 99 years in exchange for land near Springville;\nWHEREAS, the City supports prompt, lawful cleanup and remediation of the Property, restoration of\nits shoreline and habitat, and safe public access to Utah Lake;\nWHEREAS, the proposed exchange would substantially contradict years of published City planning,\nincluding Vineyard’s 2019 General Plan and 2022 North Waterfront Plan, both of which envision\npublic open space, trails, shoreline access, and low-impact recreation at or near the ponds;\nWHEREAS, the September 2026 Site-Specific Plan identifies needed cleanup and access\nimprovements but does not establish that private development of the Property is necessary to\naccomplish them;\nWHEREAS, the proposed exchange outlines easements and trails which would provide routes\nthrough and along the Property, but reserves most of the land for future private development, and\nleaves substantially less space for public use and limits the State’s management opportunities as\n\nVineyard grows; and\nWHEREAS, the public interest in a restored urban lakefront includes the ability to manage the land\nitself for recreation, habitat, heritage, and future needs, not merely passage across it;\n\nNOW, THEREFORE, BE IT RESOLVED BY THE CITY COUNCIL OF VINEYARD, UTAH, AS FOLLOWS:\nSection 1. Opposition to the exchange. The Council opposes the proposed exchange and 99-year\nprivate development lease. FFSL should cancel the exchange. Cleaning up public land does not\nrequire placing most of it under private control for nearly a century; the Site-Specific Plan has not\ndemonstrated otherwise.\nSection 2. Public management alternative. The Council calls on FFSL to retain the Property and work\nwith Vineyard City on a management plan for remediation, shoreline restoration, habitat, meaningful\npublic use, and interpretation of Geneva Steel history. FFSL should evaluate that alternative against\nthe exchange using the Property’s foreseeable condition after cleanup, not only its present industrial\ncondition, and account for the City’s General Plan and waterfront design work. The City is prepared\nto participate in that planning.\nSection 3. Heritage and access. The management plan should consider Councilmember Jacob\nWood’s Geneva Heritage Park concept. A public landscape interpreting the site’s steelmaking\nhistory and World War II contributions would have a clearer connection to that history than\nresidential buildings or a surf park—the only potential uses developer representatives have publicly\nidentified for this Property. The Council further finds that trails and access easements, while useful,\ncannot replace the public’s opportunity to use and shape the larger shoreline parcel over time. Page 56 of 57","ocr":false},{"page":57,"text":"Section 4. RDA remediation funding. If FFSL nevertheless approves the exchange, the Council calls\non the Vineyard Redevelopment Agency to deny discretionary funding for remediation that would\nfacilitate the proposed long-term private development of the Property. The RDA must evaluate its\nexisting agreements and legal obligations and vote separately; this Council resolution does not\namend or terminate any RDA commitment.\nSection 5. Transmittal and effective date. The City Recorder shall transmit this resolution to FFSL for\ninclusion in the record on the Site-Specific Plan and proposed exchange. This resolution takes effect\nupon adoption.\n\nPASSED AND ADOPTED by the Vineyard City Council this ___ day of __________, 2026.\n__________________________________\n\nZack Stratton, Mayor\n\nATTEST:\n__________________________________\n\nRobin Bond, City Recorder\n\nYES NO ABSENT ABSTAIN\n\nJacob Holdaway _____ _____ _______ ________\n\nDavid Lauret _____ _____ _______ ________\n\nParker McCumber _____ _____ _______ ________\n\nChip Price _____ _____ _______ ________\nJacob Wood _____ _____ _______ ________ Page 57 of 57","ocr":false}]